---
/*
  Meta's tracking pixel, off unless Chris switches it on.

  HOW IT IS CONTROLLED

  One field in Studio: Site Settings → Brand & contact → Meta Pixel ID. Empty is
  the default and means NO SCRIPT AT ALL - not a disabled one, not a stub,
  nothing in the page. Paste the ID from Events Manager and it starts loading on
  the next deploy. Clear it and it stops. No code change either way, which
  matters because "is the tracker on?" should be answerable by looking at Studio
  rather than by reading a template.

  NEVER ON THE PREVIEW BUILD

  The preview is a public URL that Chris, I, and every CI audit hit dozens of
  times a day. Firing the pixel there would fill his Events Manager with traffic
  that is not customers, and the audiences built from it would be junk. Guarded
  on IS_PREVIEW rather than on trust.

  WHAT THIS COSTS, STATED WHERE IT IS INCURRED

  Before this, the site set zero cookies and loaded zero third-party scripts.
  With an ID set, Meta receives every visitor's IP, page and timestamp whether or
  not they use Facebook, and fbevents.js is roughly 70 KB on every page.

  That is a real change and the privacy policy has to keep up with it. The policy
  reads the SAME field, so it describes tracking exactly when tracking exists -
  the two cannot drift into the policy claiming something the site stopped doing,
  or worse, not mentioning something it started.

  NOT A CONSENT BANNER

  This fires on load. For visitors in the EU or UK, doing that without prior
  consent is not compliant, and a banner is a bigger piece of work than a script
  tag - it needs to block the pixel until a choice is made, remember the choice,
  and offer a way to change it. If the site starts drawing meaningful EU traffic
  that becomes a real gap, and it is written down here rather than discovered.

  `is:inline` on both tags: Astro must not bundle or rewrite Meta's snippet, and
  the noscript pixel has to reach the browser exactly as written.
*/
import { stripStegaKey } from '../lib/stega';

interface Props {
  /** From siteSettings.metaPixelId. Anything non-numeric is ignored. */
  id?: unknown;
}

/*
  Stripped and validated, not trusted. A stega-contaminated ID would produce a
  pixel URL Meta silently drops - the tag would be present, the data would go
  nowhere, and nothing would say so. The digits check also means a pasted URL or
  a stray quote turns the pixel OFF rather than emitting a broken script tag.
*/
const raw = stripStegaKey(Astro.props.id);
const pixelId = /^\d{8,20}$/.test(raw) ? raw : '';

if (raw && !pixelId) {
  console.warn(
    `[MetaPixel] siteSettings.metaPixelId is "${raw}", which is not a plain ` +
      `numeric ID, so no pixel was emitted. Events Manager shows it as a ` +
      `15-16 digit number under the dataset name.`,
  );
}

const IS_PREVIEW = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_IS_PREVIEW === 'true';
const enabled = Boolean(pixelId) && !IS_PREVIEW;

if (pixelId && IS_PREVIEW) {
  console.log('[MetaPixel] ID set, but this is the preview build - not loading it.');
}
---

{enabled && (
  <>
    <script is:inline define:vars={{ pixelId }}>
      !(function (f, b, e, v, n, t, s) {
        if (f.fbq) return;
        n = f.fbq = function () {
          n.callMethod ? n.callMethod.apply(n, arguments) : n.queue.push(arguments);
        };
        if (!f._fbq) f._fbq = n;
        n.push = n;
        n.loaded = !0;
        n.version = '2.0';
        n.queue = [];
        t = b.createElement(e);
        t.async = !0;
        t.src = v;
        s = b.getElementsByTagName(e)[0];
        s.parentNode.insertBefore(t, s);
      })(window, document, 'script', 'https://connect.facebook.net/en_US/fbevents.js');
      fbq('init', pixelId);
      fbq('track', 'PageView');
    </script>
    <noscript>
      <img
        height="1"
        width="1"
        style="display:none"
        alt=""
        src={`https://www.facebook.com/tr?id=${pixelId}&ev=PageView&noscript=1`}
      />
    </noscript>
  </>
)}
